---
title: "Dynamic programming, one cell at a time"
description: "Edit distance, traced into a live 2-D grid: every cell fills in exactly the order the algorithm computes it, each fill reading the neighbours it depends on."
---

A dynamic-programming table is usually explained with a finished grid and an arrow
pointing at three neighbouring cells. Here the grid is not finished: it fills in, one
cell at a time, in the exact order the algorithm computes it, and the cells a step just
read tint differently from the cell it just wrote.

::: {.debug name="edit-distance"}
```{python}
#| trace: true
def edit_distance(a, b):
    n, m = len(a), len(b)
    dp = [[0] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]
    for i in range(n + 1):
        dp[i][0] = i
    for j in range(m + 1):
        dp[0][j] = j
    for i in range(1, n + 1):
        for j in range(1, m + 1):
            if a[i - 1] == b[j - 1]:
                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1]
            else:
                dp[i][j] = 1 + min(dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1], dp[i - 1][j - 1])
    return dp[n][m]

edit_distance("kitten", "sitting")
```
:::

`dp` is a list of lists, so it renders as the **grid** view: rows down, columns across.
Step forward and watch the write tint move cell by cell in row order, always landing on
a cell whose three dependencies (directly above, directly left, and diagonally
above-left) were already filled before it ever needed them. The bottom-right cell holds
the answer once the trace finishes.
